		WRTC

WRTC is an interpreted language which exists primarily to output bytes.

	USAGE

wrtc <infile> <outfile>

	PARSING CONVENTIONS

A program consists of a linear array of tokens. Each token is one of two kinds:
a regular token, and a literal token. A regular token is simply retrieved from
the first non-whitespace character after the last token, to the first
whitespace character following. A literal token is instead retrieved upon
reaching a quotation mark (") and consumes characters until the following
quotation mark.

The major difference between tokens is that literals may contain spaces and
lowercase letters, while a regular token may not contain spaces and is
converted to uppercase before execution.

	COMMAND LIST

The execution of a program consists of reading a 'leading token' and then
consuming a command-dependent number of tokens in the execution thereof.
What follows is a list of commands:

	REMARK <token>
		Skips itself and the token which follows it.

		EXAMPLE		Does nothing

			REMARK "
				This remark is multiline because literal tokens
				do not check the whitespace inside them. You
				would however require \ in the remark for a
				double quote to be allowed (i.e., \")
			"

	BLOCK
		Creates a new block in the memory of the output, if the token
		following BLOCK is FROM, then it will have the form:

		BLOCK FROM <value> TO <value>

		And will describe the initial address (in the output) and the
		final address (in the output) of the block. If nothing follows
		the leaing BLOCK, then FROM will be the value of the current
		block's TO (or zero in the case that this is the first block)
		and TO will be set to zero (and redecided upon a following
		END-BLOCK).

		The current block is set to this new block.

		EXAMPLE		Creates a bootable binary which halts.
			BLOCK FROM 0 TO 1FE
				WRITE 0F4 0EB 0FD THEN
			END-BLOCK

			BLOCK
				WRITE 55 0AA THEN
			END-BLOCK

	END-BLOCK
		If the current block's TO value is less than or equal to it's
		FROM value, it shall be updated to the length of the data of
		the block plus the FROM value of the block.

		The current block is set to None.

		EXAMPLE		Creates a block of zero size

			BLOCK
			END-BLOCK

	WRITE <values...> THEN
		Followed by a list of values terminated with THEN, each of
		these values are bitwise-anded with 0xFF and added to the data
		of the current_block. If there is no current_block, it is
		undefinded behaviour.

		EXAMPLE		Writes 'HELLO WORLD' to the file

			BLOCK
				WRITE 48 45 4C 4C 4F 20 57 THEN
			END-BLOCK

	DECLARE
		If directly following is GLOBAL then the value declared is
		declared in the topmost scope. If it is LOCAL then it is
		in the current scope, LOCAL is default and need not be
		specified.

		If following the optional scope specified, CONSTANT is
		specified, in the case of a variable, it will be declared as
		constant and thus cannot change. In the case of a procedure
		it defines that WRITE syntax shall be used. However if it is
		MUTABLE, the variable shall be mutable and the procedure shall
		use BLOCK syntax. Think of the procedure CONST/MUT dichotomy
		as describing whether or not the procedure may rely on
		variables or stack. MUTABLE is default.

		Following the optional scope and mutability specified, there
		*must* be either VARIABLE or PROCEDURE present.

		If variable is present, the whole expression is of the
		following form:

			DECLARE <scope> <mutability> VARIABLE <name> AS <value>

		If procedure is present and it is MUTABLE the expression is
		as follows:

			DECLARE <scope> [MUTABLE] PROCEDURE <name>
				<tokens...>
			END-PROCEDURE

		Defining the procedure as the list of tokens specified.

		If instead, the procedure is CONSTANT then the following is
		the expression:

			DECLARE <scope> CONSTANT PROCEDURE <name> AS
				<values...> THEN

		Which defines the procedure as though it where a WRITE
		statement containing all tokens following the AS.

		EXAMPLE		Creates a variable and two procedures which
				have the same function of writing HELLO to the
				output.

			DECLARE GLOBAL CONSTANT VARIABLE CONST AS 4E

			DECLARE GLOBAL PROCEDURE HELLO
				WRITE 48 45 4C 4C 4F THEN
			END-PROCEDURE

			DECLARE GLOBAL CONSTANT PROCEDURE HELLO2 AS 48 45 4C
				4C 4F THEN

	MOVE <value> INTO <name>
		Sets the MUTABLE variable named to the value specified.

	( <values...> )
		Pops the values from the stack.

		EXAMPLE		A procedure which writes its input from the
				stack.

			DECLARE PROCEDURE OUT
				( INPUT )
				WRITE INPUT THEN
			END-PROCEDURE

	<token> <values...> THEN
		If the command given is unknown and consititutes a previously
		DECLARED PROCEDURE then the values shall be pushed to the stack
		in reverse order and the procedure will be executed.

		EXAMPLE		Calls the procedure from the () example writing
				0FF to the output.

			BLOCK
				OUT 0FF THEN
			END-BLOCK

	IF <value> IS [NOT] ZERO <tokens...> [ELSE <tokens...>] END-IF
		Checks if the value is zero and executes the tokens before
		END-IF/ELSE if that value is/is not zero according to the
		presence of the NOT symbol.

		If the statement does not run the 'if code' then the code
		after ELSE will run if present.

		EXAMPLE		A procedure which outputs 0 if passed a 1
				the stack and a 1 if passed a zero.

			DECLARE PROCEDURE NOT ( INPUT )
				IF INPUT IS NOT ZERO
					WRITE 00 THEN
				ELSE
					WRITE 01 THEN
				END-IF
			END-PROCEDURE

	INCLUDE <token>
		Reads the file specified and executes it in place with the
		current scope, such that it would be if it were in the file
		at the current location.

		EXAMPLE		Including the OUT procedure from the () example
				from a file out.wrt and executing it.

			INCLUDE "out.wrt"

			BLOCK
				OUT 0FF THEN
			END-BLOCK

	$ ...
		The following token is read as namespaced and executed
		accordingly. We shall from hereon refer to namespaced
		commands with a prefixed '$'.

	$ ADD <value> TO <name>
		Adds the value specified to the MUTABLE variable named.

		EXAMPLE		Add 10 to X.

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 2
			$ ADD 0A TO X
			REMARK "X is now 12 (0C)"

	$ SUBTRACT <value> FROM <name>
		Subtracts the value specified from the MUTABLE variable named.

		EXAMPLE		Subtract 10 from X.

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 0C
			$ SUBTRACT 0A FROM X
			REMARK "X is now 2"

	$ MULTIPLY <name> BY <value>
		Multiplies the MUTABLE variable named by the value specified.

		EXAMPLE		Multiply X by 2.

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 06
			$ MULTIPLY X BY 02
			REMARK "X is now 12 (0C)"

	$ DIVIDE <name> BY <value>
		Divides the MUTABLE variable named by the value specified.

		EXAMPLE		Divide X by 2.

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 18
			$ DIVIDE X BY 02
			REMARK "X is now 12 (0C)"

	$ MODULO <value> OF <name>
		Retrieves the remainder of ((MUTABLE name) mod value).

		EXAMPLE		Get the remainder of 32 / 3

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 32
			$ MODULO 3 OF X
			REMARK "X is now 2"

	$ AND <value> WITH <name>
		Bitwise ands the MUTABLE variable named with the value
		specified.

		EXAMPLE		X & 4

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 0C
			$ AND 04 WITH X
			REMARK "X is now 04"

	$ OR <value> WITH <name>
		Bitwise ors the MUTABLE variable named with the value
		specified.

		EXAMPLE		X | 2

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 0C
			$ OR 02 WITH X
			REMARK "X is now 14 (0E)"

	$ XOR <value> WITH <name>
		Bitwise xors the MUTABLE variable named with the value
		specified.

		EXAMPLE		X | 2

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 0C
			$ XOR 00 WITH X
			$ AND 0FF WITH X
			REMARK "X is now 243 (0F3)"

	$ SHL <name> BY <value>
		Shifts the MUTABLE variable named left by the value specified.

		EXAMPLE		X << 2

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 03
			$ SHL X BY 2
			REMARK "X is now 12 (0C)"

	$ SHR <name> BY <value>
		Shifts the MUTABLE variable named right by the value specified.

		EXAMPLE		X >> 2

			DECLARE VARIABLE X AS 30
			$ SHL X BY 2
			REMARK "X is now 12 (0C)"

	$ PUSH <value>
		Pushes the value to the stack.

		EXAMPLE		Call a procedure with arguments (OUT) without
				any values in the parameter list.

			BLOCK
				$ PUSH 0FF
				OUT THEN
			END-BLOCK

	$ POP [INTO <name>]
		Pops a value of the stack, if INTO <name> is present, the
		corresponding variable will have the value popped into it.

		EXAMPLE		Declare OUT without use of the ( command.

			DECLARE PROCEDURE OUT
				DECLARE VARIABLE INPUT

				$ POP INTO INPUT
				WRITE INPUT THEN
			END-PROCEDURE
